<BaseLayout title="About">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="simple">
      <h1>About</h1>
      <p>I am Kokou. Eight-plus years of SRE and platform work, the last few on data and ML infrastructure: Kubernetes clusters with GPUs, model serving, the pipelines that feed them, and the observability that tells you which one is on fire.</p>
      <p>At work the hardware is someone else's. At home it is one RTX 5060 with 8 GB, a six-core Ryzen, and WSL2. I decided to measure on that rather than wait for access to something bigger, and to be explicit about what transfers. Memory pressure, scheduler behaviour, KV-cache arithmetic, device plugin and DRA semantics, DCGM's field set: all of that is the same physics on a consumer card, it just arrives at batch 4 instead of batch 128. NVLink, MIG, and anything inter-node do not exist here, and each post says so in one sentence when it matters.</p>
      <p>One opinion, so you know where I stand: most inference latency problems I have been called in on were scheduler problems wearing a memory problem's clothes. Look at the queue depth before you look at the allocator.</p>
      <p>Stack, in the order I reach for it: Kubernetes (EKS, GKE, and k3s on this desk) with the NVIDIA GPU Operator, Kueue for admission, vLLM and llama.cpp for serving, Ray for anything that does not fit in one pod, Terraform and ArgoCD underneath, DCGM and Prometheus and a growing amount of eBPF on top. Python and Go for the glue.</p>
      <p>Contact: <a href="mailto:hello@kokou.dev">hello@kokou.dev</a>, <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/kinfinity3/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">LinkedIn</a>, <a href="https://github.com/kokou-egbewatt" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub</a>.</p>
      <p class="cap">This site is static, built with Astro from the same repo as the harnesses. The interactive pieces are plain JavaScript. Type is Newsreader, Inter, and IBM Plex Mono.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
